# Robot hand motion control

When controlling the robot hand we have the following options: 
we can make the robot hand track kinematic state of player hand, track position, velocity or acceleration
all of this choices are bad, they suffer from the same issue, robot hand can interact with game world
objects which will constraint position, change velocity and acceleration, these changes are not reflected
in player hand motion, so both motions are now out of sync.
Second option is to make player input a force applied to the robot hand, the same as any other force
(reactions from game world collisions etc ...), this way we do not have a problem of kinematic state
being out of sync.
since we want to apply a force we need to get a vector from player hand controller, we pick the natural
choice of controller linear velocity. (velocity in what exact frame of reference is disscussed
in (Note: Robot motion controller mapping)

# Unreal Physics engine stuff:

## Collision meshes
unreal engine by default will create a collision manifold based on object mesh, we should not rely on that,
we can add simlple convex shapes to act as our object collision mesh (toolbar menu of mesh editor)
also, only a few contact points will be used per such mesh, so adding more simple convex shapes to our
mesh collision geometry means we get hopefully correct contact points (can be debugged in Chaos debugger view
show top menu > collision)
we can add convex simple collision meshes from external modeling tool an import them as UCX_<mesh_name>

## Inertial conditioning
Inertial conditioning adjusts inertial tensors in non-physical ways for more solver stability (per chatgpt)
it our tested in resulted in very noticiable violation of (linear) momentum conservation and
runaway spinning even when force was applied just for a breif time


Trying to understand Chaos interals:

PBDRigidsSolver : big entry point for physics scene (rigid body related)
PBDRigidsEvolutionGBF: calls IslandGroupManager.Solve
IslandGroupManager.cpp: Solve implementation, calls solve for each IslandGroup
IslandGroup: a set of constraints to be solved sequentially, subclass of FPBDConstraintGroupSolver
these thing does not solve the constraint, it has bunch of containers for things and tracks what
solvers to use.
There is a FConstraintContainerSolver (SolverConstraintContainer.h), (general type) per constraint type, per island
TPBDJointContainerSolver (PBDJointContainerSolver.h) is the subclass for joints.
it holds a FPBDJointConstraints which is the Constraint container
it holds an array of JointSolverType (template name, actual type can be FPBDJointSolver)
All the AddConstaint(s) talks about particules, but particules are general term for things used in
the physics engine.

FPBDJointSolver (PBDJointSolverGaussSeidel.h):
holds two solver bodies (array of two FConstraintSolverBody)
contains the actual constraint solver code (it uses ComputeJointFactorMatrix (Utilities.h) or an ispc version)

FSolverBody (SolverBody.h):
struct that contains particule state, used only by solvers during constraint solve, to read state and
accumulate results, there is gather/scatter mechanism to read/write this data from its original SOA form
FConstraintSolverBody: FSolverBody reference + mass information

current hypthesis: there is one container, TPBDJointContainerSolver, (per thread ? per island ?) for all joints
there is a FPBDJointSolver for each joint in the container.

FPBDJointSettings (PBDJointConstraintTypes.h): contains the joint defining data, degrees of freedom, limits etc ...

